/*  Four design patterns, and what is left of them once functions are values.

    A pattern is an answer to a question the language asks. Half the classic catalogue
    answers questions Cufet does not ask, so those are not in this file — writing them
    here would mean writing around the language instead of in it:

        Visitor       exists because most languages cannot dispatch on a type.
                      `Judge` over a closed union does it, and checks the arms are
                      complete — see `examples/language/dispatch.cufe`.
        Null Object   exists to dodge a null. `voidable` will not hand you one, and
                      narrowing makes the check the compiler's business.
        Singleton     is `permanently`.
        Iterator      is a stash — see `examples/language/stashes.cufe`.

    The four below survive, because each one arranges behaviour rather than working
    around a missing feature. Underneath they are one idea wearing four hats: a
    function is a value, so it can be stored, passed, wrapped, and kept in a list.

        Command     a function stored in an object, with a name attached to it
        Strategy    a function taken as a parameter, so the caller picks the rule
        Decorator   a function taken and a NEW one handed back, wrapping the old
        Observer    a series of functions, every one of them called in turn

    ★ The thing to watch is the type. `the number function apply given (the number)` is a
    field holding a function from a number to a number, and `void function given (the text)`
    is one taking text and answering nothing. These are checked exactly as every other type
    is, so handing over the wrong shape of function is refused where it is written rather
    than discovered halfway through a checkout.
*/

// ── Command: the action, with a name on it ──────────────────────────────────────────────

// A charge knows what it is called and what it does to a running total. That is the entire
// pattern — the ACTION is data, so a bill is an ordinary series that can be built, held,
// reordered and printed like any other series.
Define object charge with (the text label, the number function apply given (the number)).

Bind number to gift-wrap, given (the number total): Return total + 2.50. Done.
Bind number to loyalty, given (the number total): Return total - 5. Done.
Bind number to sales-tax, given (the number total): Return total * 1.10. Done.

// ── Strategy: the caller picks the rule ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Three postage rules with one shape between them. Nothing here knows about the others, and
// the checkout below knows about none of them — it takes whichever one it is handed.
Bind number to flat-postage, given (the number total): Return 4.95. Done.
Bind number to collected, given (the number total): Return 0. Done.
Bind number to free-over-fifty, given (the number total):
    If total is greater than 50, return 0.
    Return 4.95.
Done.

// ── Decorator: a rule in, a different rule out ──────────────────────────────────────────

/*  Both of these take a postage rule and hand back another one of the same type, so a
    decorated rule is accepted anywhere a bare one is and can be decorated again. The
    returned function is a CLOSURE: `limited` is declared inside `capped-at` and captures
    both `ceiling` and `inner`, which outlive the call that made it.

    ★ Note what is NOT here — no interface to implement, no wrapper class holding a
    reference to the thing it wraps, and nothing for the wrapped rule to know about. The
    pattern is what is left after a language stops needing an object to carry a function. */
Bind number function given (the number) to capped-at, given (the number ceiling, the number function inner given (the number)):
    Bind number to limited, given (the number total):
        Define asked as cast inner on (total).
        If asked is greater than ceiling, return ceiling.
        Return asked.
    Done.
    Return limited.
Done.

Bind number function given (the number) to announced, given (the text name, the number function inner given (the number)):
    Bind number to telling, given (the number total):
        Define answer as cast inner on (total).
        State "      ({name} asks {answer converted to text})".
        Return answer.
    Done.
    Return telling.
Done.

// ── Observer: a list of functions, all of them called ───────────────────────────────────

/*  Two watchers with nothing to do with each other. One reports every step; the other stays
    quiet until the total passes a threshold. Neither knows the checkout exists, and the
    checkout knows only that it holds a series of functions taking text.

    ★ `the series of void function given (the text) watchers` is the whole of it — no wrapper
    object, no interface for a watcher to implement, nothing to register with. Adding one is
    `Insert`, and having none is an empty series.

    ⚠ Where the name sits is what says which type this is, and the two readings are both real:

        the series of number function steps given (the number)   a function RETURNING a series
        the series of number function given (the number) steps   a SERIES of functions

    The `given` clause is the divider. A function element type states its own clause, so the
    name has nowhere to go but after the whole type. */
Bind void to narrate, given (the text note): State "    {note}". Done.
Bind void to flag-large, given (the text note):
    If note contains "*", State "    ^ over fifty".
Done.

// ── The checkout, which knows none of the above by name ─────────────────────────────────

Bind number to check-out, given (
        the number subtotal,
        the series of charge bill,
        the number function postage-rule given (the number),
        the series of void function given (the text) watchers):
    Define running as subtotal.
    For each step in bill, repeat:
        The running becomes cast the apply of step on (running).
        Define mark as "" when running is not greater than 50, otherwise " *".
        For each listener in watchers, repeat:
            Cast listener on ("{the label of step} -> {running converted to text}{mark}").
        Done.
    Done.
    Return running + cast postage-rule on (running).
Done.

Pull a rabbit.
    // The bill is built once and reused. Because a charge is a value, the same series drives
    // every checkout below — only the postage rule changes.
    Define bill as a series of charge with (
        a new charge { the label "gift wrap", the apply gift-wrap },
        a new charge { the label "loyalty  ", the apply loyalty },
        a new charge { the label "sales tax", the apply sales-tax }).

    Define watchers as a series of void function given (the text) with (narrate, flag-large).

    Define quiet as a series of void function given (the text).

    // Watched, so the steps are visible. `quiet` below is the same parameter with nothing in
    // it — no watcher is a perfectly good number of watchers.
    State "40.00, watched step by step".
    State "  total, flat postage     {cast check-out on (40, bill, flat-postage, watchers) converted to text}".

    // The same bill and the same checkout under each rule. Only the argument changes.
    State "".
    State "the same bill, one rule at a time".
    State "  collected               {cast check-out on (40, bill, collected, quiet) converted to text}".
    State "  free over fifty, 40.00  {cast check-out on (40, bill, free-over-fifty, quiet) converted to text}".
    State "  free over fifty, 60.00  {cast check-out on (60, bill, free-over-fifty, quiet) converted to text}".
    State "  flat, 60.00             {cast check-out on (60, bill, flat-postage, quiet) converted to text}".

    // Watched again at 60.00, where the second watcher finally has something to say.
    State "".
    State "60.00, watched — the second watcher only speaks over fifty".
    State "  total, free over fifty  {cast check-out on (60, bill, free-over-fifty, watchers) converted to text}".

    // A decorated rule is a rule: same slot, and one can wrap the other. The line the wrapper
    // prints comes from the DECORATOR, not from a watcher — `quiet` is empty here.
    State "".
    State "the same flat rule, wrapped".
    Define capped as cast capped-at on (3, flat-postage).
    Define loud as cast announced on ("capped flat", capped).
    State "  capped at 3.00          {cast check-out on (40, bill, capped, quiet) converted to text}".
    State "  and announced           {cast check-out on (40, bill, loud, quiet) converted to text}".
Done.
